1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:08,953
IETF 127 Hackathon: installing the CoAP-over-BPv7 Wireshark dissector.

2
00:00:10,403 --> 00:00:18,969
In this tutorial, we install our project's Wireshark Lua dissector on the Satellite: the same plugin we already use on Earth.

3
00:00:19,019 --> 00:00:27,759
It follows CoAP-over-BPv7 Draft-05, on IPN services 64, 10 and 11.

4
00:00:27,809 --> 00:00:38,191
It classifies CoAP Single and Aggregate bundles, and decodes the 24-bit MID, Tokens, the Payload-length option and the CoAP payload.

5
00:00:38,241 --> 00:00:45,070
The terminal screens are illustrations; the Wireshark screenshots are real, from the Satellite VM.

6
00:00:46,470 --> 00:00:51,999
Step 1: we reuse the existing file from our project repository on GitHub.

7
00:00:52,049 --> 00:00:56,422
It is the Lua dissector in the repository's wireshark folder.

8
00:00:56,472 --> 00:01:00,728
Use the same source file; there is no need to create a new dissector.

9
00:01:02,128 --> 00:01:04,997
Step 2: open a terminal on the Satellite.

10
00:01:05,047 --> 00:01:10,103
All of these commands run on the Satellite VM, not on the Rover or Earth.

11
00:01:11,503 --> 00:01:17,949
Step 3: create your local Wireshark plugins folder, then list it to confirm it exists.

12
00:01:17,999 --> 00:01:22,033
The folder lives in your home directory, so no sudo is needed.

13
00:01:23,433 --> 00:01:31,244
Step 4: download the original Lua file from the project repository with wget, straight into the plugins folder.

14
00:01:31,294 --> 00:01:37,833
The command is split across lines here for readability, so please copy the exact command from the README.

15
00:01:39,233 --> 00:01:43,538
Step 5: list the plugins folder, to check that the file is there.

16
00:01:43,588 --> 00:01:47,508
You should see one Lua file, about 6.8 kilobytes.

17
00:01:48,908 --> 00:01:53,379
Step 6: close and restart Wireshark, so that it loads the plugin.

18
00:01:53,429 --> 00:02:00,466
Then open the Single demo capture, single_demo.pcapng, from the Satellite evidence folder.

19
00:02:00,516 --> 00:02:05,447
This is a real Satellite screenshot, taken after the plugin was installed.

20
00:02:06,847 --> 00:02:11,682
Step 7: type bpv7 in the green display-filter bar.

21
00:02:11,732 --> 00:02:16,404
Wireshark now shows only the four bundle observations from the demo.

22
00:02:17,804 --> 00:02:21,142
Step 8: confirm that the Lua dissector has loaded.

23
00:02:21,192 --> 00:02:25,928
The Info column should read CoAP/BP Single, one message.

24
00:02:25,979 --> 00:02:31,041
And the packet details now include a CoAP over BPv7 section.

25
00:02:32,441 --> 00:02:38,303
Step 9: expand CoAP over BPv7, and then CoAP member #1.

26
00:02:38,353 --> 00:02:41,151
Verify that it is a Non-confirmable GET,

27
00:02:41,201 --> 00:02:43,280
with a 24-bit message ID,

28
00:02:43,330 --> 00:02:45,768
and that there is no Payload-length option.

29
00:02:47,168 --> 00:02:52,093
Step 10: the same plugin also provides filters for later experiments.

30
00:02:52,143 --> 00:02:54,641
bpv7 shows all bundles.

31
00:02:54,691 --> 00:03:01,348
coapbp05.count == 1 selects bundles carrying one CoAP message,

32
00:03:01,398 --> 00:03:05,231
and coapbp05.count == 2 selects Aggregate bundles carrying two.

33
00:03:05,281 --> 00:03:10,038
The IPN destination services are 64, 10 and 11.

34
00:03:10,088 --> 00:03:21,068
No application or µD3TN restart is needed: the dissector only changes how Wireshark displays existing BPv7 payloads.

35
00:03:22,518 --> 00:03:29,948
Thank you for watching. The dissector and the exact commands are in the project repository and its README.
